          "type": "text",
          "text": "You are a research subagent in the repo /Users/leonchan/WorkSpace/IDX-Tic-Tac-Toe (it currently contains only \"AI Dev Test - Candidate brief.md\" and .git). Read that brief first. This is research only: do NOT write game code beyond throwaway solver scripts, do NOT create/modify docs/RULES.md or docs/DESIGN.md, and do NOT run any git commit/add.\n\nTHE DESIGN QUESTION\nCan 3x3 tic-tac-toe (two players, X and O, alternating, recognisably tic-tac-toe) be modified so that (MUST) no terminal state is a draw and (MUST) every line of play terminates; and (NICE) the rule is simple and first/second player win rates are similar? We need evidence to choose a rule set, which will later be written to docs/RULES.md and defended in docs/DESIGN.md.\n\nDELIVERABLE\nExactly ONE Markdown file in the repo: docs/research/no-draw-variants.md. Save nothing else in the repo; put solver scripts in /var/folders/9m/zqylzxwx66dfn_m5fyzjl93w0000gn/T/opencode and embed the essential solver code in the Markdown (fenced block) so every number is reproducible. Every non-obvious claim needs a citation to the source that owns it. Mark anything you could not trace as \"unverified\" and say what you tried.\n\nMETHOD — PART 1: your own exhaustive solvers (treat these as primary evidence)\nWrite stdlib-only Python that exhaustively enumerates the full game tree (3^9 states; all legal move sequences, stopping at terminal states). For EACH rule set below report: (a) count of terminal draws (must be 0 for a valid candidate; for standard rules report the actual count), (b) max and mean game length, (c) perfect-play value (first or second player forced win) plus the winning first moves, (d) outcome distribution over all legal terminal sequences under uniform random play (report counts AND percentages; note the classic 255,168 total for standard rules), (e) same distribution under a simple greedy policy (win now if possible, else block opponent's immediate win, else random) as a rough human proxy. State clearly that this is exhaustive enumeration, i.e. a proof, not a sample. Also verify that no candidate allows a reachable cycle (termination argument).\nRule sets (cells 0-8, X moves first, no passing):\n  A. Standard: own 3-in-a-row wins; if board fills with no line, the player who made the last move wins.\n  B. Standard: own 3-in-a-row wins; if board fills with no line, the player who made the last move loses (equivalently O wins a filled board; in 3x3 the last move on a full board is always X).\n  C. Misere: completing your own 3-in-a-row loses; if board fills, last mover wins.\n  D. Misere: completing your own 3-in-a-row loses; if board fills, last mover loses.\n  E. \"Any filled line wins\": a move wins if it makes ANY row/column/diagonal fully occupied, regardless of symbol; no fill tiebreak. (Report the max game length bound.)\n  F. Standard, but O is pre-placed on the centre before X's first move; solve with (i) last mover wins a fill, (ii) last mover loses a fill. This tests whether a handicap improves balance.\nAlso solve standard rules (no modification) to confirm perfect-play draw and the 255,168 split. If a rule set is ambiguous, state your interpretation.\n\nMETHOD — PART 2: literature, primary sources only (papers, books, official docs, OEIS; not blogs/wikis as the owner of a claim — chase each claim to the source that owns it and quote a key line)\n  - Standard 3x3 tic-tac-toe perfect-play value and the classic 255,168 game count with its outcome split: find the original/owning source; if none is traceable, say so and rely on your own enumeration.\n  - Full-board no-line positions: how many exist, and confirm by exhaustive reachability that they are reachable in normal play; explain why this forces every no-draw variant to add a tiebreak or change the win condition.\n  - Misere/avoidance tic-tac-toe (make a line to lose): known results, especially the first player's centre + 180-degree-rotation mirror strategy.\n  - Notakto (both players place X, misere): known result on a single 3x3 board, primary source.\n  - Maker-Breaker tic-tac-toe (Maker first tries to complete a line; Breaker wins if the board fills with no Maker line; draws impossible by definition): known result for 3x3, primary source (e.g. Beck, \"Combinatorial Games: Tic-Tac-Toe Theory\"; Maker-Breaker surveys).\n  - Zermelo determinacy for finite perfect-information games: the source that owns it, and the consequence that perfect-play 50/50 balance is impossible in any no-draw variant, so \"balance\" can only be empirical or match-level.\n  - Pie/swap rule (Gale 1979, Hex; also used in Havannah etc.): how it balances first-player advantage, and the argument that in a no-draw game a perfect second player can simply swap and take the winning side, so it does not create theoretical fairness.\n\nMETHOD — PART 3: per-candidate verdict\nFor each rule set: how many sentences it takes to state (simplicity), MEASURED first/second win rates, perfect-play winner, no-draw status, termination bound, and a keep/reject verdict with reasons. Call out which candidates are dominated.\n\nRETURN TO ME (max ~40 lines): the file path; a one-line verdict per candidate (draws impossible? max length; perfect-play winner; random-play split; where the result comes from); your top recommendation with caveats; whether the classic 255,168 numbers were reproduced; and any \"unverified\" items.",
