r(turn), depth+1)\n            for k in c2: counts[k]+=c2[k]\n            maxd=max(maxd,md); sumd+=sd\n            for k in p2: probs[k]+=p2[k]/n\n        return (counts, maxd, sumd, probs)\n    ...\n```\nHmm, recursion depth ≤ 9, fine. But the return of counts should be per-outcome where draws use key None. Let me use key 0 for draw maybe. And distinguish (winner X), (winner O), (draw).\n\nNote: rec depends on depth? No! The state outcome and subtree structure are independent of depth; depth is determined by stone count. So memoization on (b,turn) is fine, and I can compute depth from stone count for length. Let me compute length as number of stones minus initial stones (for F). I'll pass length via popcount. Let me instead define: game length = number of moves played (stones added after the starting position). For standard start empty: length = popcount(b). For F: popcount(b) - 1.\n\nSo rec returns (counts, max_len, sum_len, probs) where max_len = max moves played from start to terminal. Compute as: at a leaf, len = popcount(b) - initial_stones. Children: max_len = max(child max_len) but leaf len computed directly. Since depth monotonic, fine.\n\nUniform probs exact with Fractions. Branching up to 9; 6000 states; fine.\n\nGreedy DP similar but with candidate policy:\n\n```python\ndef greedy(rule, b, turn):\n    last = other(turn)\n    if rule(b,last) is not None: leaf.\n    legal = empties\n    # immediate wins\n    wins = [];\n    for i in legal:\n        nb = ...\n        if rule(nb, turn)==turn: wins.append(i)\n    if wins: cand = wins\n    else:\n        safe = []\n        for i in legal:\n            nb = ...\n            if rule(nb,turn) is not None: continue  # my move ended game without me winning? e.g., misere: I completed own line -> I lose; or fill last loses under D; treat as candidate but not safe...\n            # opponent immediate win?\n            opp_win = any(rule(apply(nb,j,other(turn)), other(turn))==other(turn) for j empties(nb))\n           