e terminal losses, cand = legal → uniform random among them, but each ends the game. Fine.\n\nAlso, if some moves are terminal wins and others terminal losses, wins chosen. Good.\n\nNow, deterministic outputs. I'll output a table per rule:\n- rule name\n- draws (leaf count), X wins, O wins, total leaves, %s (leaf-share)\n- uniform-random probabilities: X%, O%, draw% (exact Fractions → float)\n- max length, mean length over leaves, expected length under uniform random\n- perfect-play value (minimax): use a separate solver returning outcome from X's perspective with optimal play; draws possible.\n- winning first moves for X: legal first moves m where minimax(child with O to move) == X. Also count first moves that force a win for X if any; if value is O, no winning first moves; report \"none (O wins with best play)\".\n- For F: first moves from the initial handicap position.\n- Greedy policy distribution: X%, O%, draw%; max/mean length under greedy as well maybe.\n\nAlso verify: \"no candidate allows a reachable cycle\": I'll do a check by tracking that each DFS path strictly increases stone count — trivially true; plus emit max depth. To make it explicit, I can assert no state has a self-loop via move application. I'll include a line in the script: `assert all(new stone count = old+1)`. And report \"max length <= 9 for all rules → every line of play terminates; the state graph is a DAG graded by stone count, no passing\". Fine.\n\nFor standard tree stats, also:\n- total distinct reachable positions (with turn) in standard play: count states visited in rec.\n- distinct terminal positions by category: X-line positions, O-line positions, full-no-line positions. Careful: X-line terminal positions include all boards where X has a line and the game could have ended at that point (reachable without a prior line). I'll enumerate: reachable states where rule terminates = terminal states; group by board (ignoring turn) → count distinct boards. Report X-line boards, O-line board