ably compact). The task says \"Include the solver code plus its raw output\". So the full raw output in a fenced block. Should be fine if I keep the output summary-focused (not per-length histograms for all). Let me include length histograms condensed as \"length:count\" lists.\n\nLet me now write solver.py.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nExhaustive solver for 3x3 tic-tac-toe no-draw rule sets.\nstdlib only. Enumerates every legal line of play to termination.\nNOTE: recursion depth <= 9, tree size <= ~10^6 nodes; runs in seconds.\n\"\"\"\nfrom fractions import Fraction\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nfrom itertools import combinations\n\nEMPTY, X, O = 0, 1, 2\nSYM = {X: \"X\", O: \"O\", None: \"draw\"}\nLINES = ((0,1,2),(3,4,5),(6,7,8),(0,3,6),(1,4,7),(2,5,8),(0,4,8),(2,4,6))\n\ndef other(p): return O if p == X else X\n\ndef mono(b, p):\n    return any(b[a] == p and b[c] == p and b[d] == p for a, c, d in LINES)\n\ndef any_full_line(b):\n    return any(b[a] != EMPTY and b[c] != EMPTY and b[d] != EMPTY for a, c, d in LINES)\n\ndef board_full(b):\n    return all(c != EMPTY for c in b)\n\n# ---- terminal rules: f(board, last_mover) -> winner (X/O) or None ----\ndef R_std_fill_wins(b, last):\n    if mono(b, X): return X\n    if mono(b, O): return O\n    return last if board_full(b) else None\n\ndef R_std_fill_loses(b, last):\n    if mono(b, X): return X\n    if mono(b, O): return O\n    return other(last) if board_full(b) else None\n\ndef R_misere_fill_wins(b, last):\n    if mono(b, last): return other(last)\n    if mono(b, other(last)): return last   # defensive; unreachable in play\n    return last if board_full(b) else None\n\ndef R_misere_fill_loses(b, last):\n    if mono(b, last): return other(last)\n    if mono(b, other(last)): return last\n    return other(last) if board_full(b) else None\n\ndef R_any_line(b, last):\n    return last if any_full_line(b) else None\n\ndef R_maker_breaker(b, last):\n    if mono(b, X): return X\n    return O if board_full(b) else None\n```\n\nWait, in R_misere, the defensive branch `if mono(b, other(last)): return last` — if the opponent's line exists and the mover didn't complete one, then opponent completed it earlier and game should already have ended. Unreachable in our DFS, but if it were reached, who won? The opponent completed a line → opponent loses → last mover wins → return last. That's what I wrote. Fine, but note it in the doc as unreachable/defensive.\n\nNow analyze():\n\n```python\ndef analyze(rule, start=None, label=\"\"):\n    if start is None:\n        start = (EMPTY,) * 9\n    start_stones = sum(1 for c in start if c != EMPTY)\n\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def rec(b, turn):\n        w = rule(b, other(turn))\n        if w is not None:\n            L = sum(1 for c in b if c != EMPTY) - start_stones\n            return ({X: 1 if w == X else 0, O: 1 if w == O else 0, None: 0},\n                    L, L, {X: Fraction(w == X), O: Fraction(w == O), None: Fraction(w is None)}, 1)\n        children = [i for i in range(9) if b[i] == EMPTY]\n        m = len(children)\n        counts = {X: 0, O: 0, None: 0}\n        probs = {X: Fraction(0), O: Fraction(0), None: Fraction(0)}\n        mx = 0; s = 0; nstates = 0\n        for i in children:\n            nb = b[:i] + (turn,) + b[i + 1:]\n            c2, m2, s2, p2, n2 = rec(nb, other(turn))\n            for k in counts: counts[k] += c2[k]\n            mx = max(mx, m2); s += s2; nstates += n2\n            for k in probs: probs[k] += p2[k] / m\n        return counts, mx, s, probs, nstates + 1\n```\nHmm, `Fraction(w == X)` — bool is int subclass; Fraction(True) = 1. Fine but let me write explicitly.\n\nAlso, the mean length over leaves = s / total_leaves.\n\nPerfect value:\n\n```python\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def value(b, turn):\n        w = rule(b, other(turn))\n        if w is not None:\n            return w\n        vals = set()\n        for i in range(9):\n            if b[i] == EMPTY:\n                nb = ...\n                vals.add(value(nb, other(turn)))\n        if turn in vals: return turn\n        if None in vals: return None\n        return other(turn)\n```\n\nWinning first moves:\n```python\n    first_moves = {}\n    for i in range(9):\n        if start[i] == EMPTY:\n            nb = start[:i] + (start_turn,) + start[i+1:]\n            first_moves[i] = value(nb, other(start_turn))\n    winning = [i for i, v in first_moves.items() if v == start_turn]\n```\nFor standard start empty, start_turn = X.\n\nGreedy:\n```python\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def greedy(b, turn):\n        w = rule(b, other(turn))\n        if w is not None:\n            L = ...\n            return ({X: Fraction(...), O: ..., None: ...}, Fraction(L), L, {X:..., O:..., None:...})\n        legal = [...]\n        def play(bb, i, p):\n            return bb[:i] + (p,) + bb[i+1:]\n        wins = [i for i in legal if rule(play(b, i, turn), turn) == turn]\n        if wins:\n            cand = wins\n        else:\n            safe = []\n            for i in legal:\n                nb = play(b, i, turn)\n                if rule(nb, turn) is not None:\n                    continue\n                opp = other(turn)\n                opp_win_now = False\n                for j in range(9):\n                    if nb[j] == EMPTY:\n                        if rule(play(nb, j, opp), opp) == opp:\n                            opp_win_now = True\n                            break\n                if not opp_win_now:\n                    safe.append(i)\n            cand = safe if safe else legal\n        m = len(cand)\n        probs = {X: Fraction(0), O: Fraction(0), None: Fraction(0)}\n        counts = {X: 0, O: 0, None: 0}\n        exp = Fraction(0)\n        mx = 0\n        for i in cand:\n            nb = play(b, i, turn)\n            p2, e2, m2, c2 = greedy(nb, other(turn))\n            for k in probs: probs[k] += p2[k] / m\n            for k in counts: counts[k] += c2[k]\n            exp += e2 / m\n            mx = max(mx, m2)\n        return probs, exp, mx, counts\n```\nReturn signature: (probs, expected_length, max_length, greedy_leaf_counts). For leaves: probs={w:1}, expected_length=L, max=L, counts={w:1}.\n\nThen a report function printing all.\n\nAlso the standard-tree terminal positions and the 16 positions:\n```python\ndef standard_terminal_and_positions():\n    # DFS from empty with std fill-wins rule (or any standard terminal check), collect distinct boards\n```\nI'll collect:\n- distinct reachable states (b, turn) count\n- distinct terminal boards (by board)\n- among terminal boards: X-line boards, O-line boards, full-no-line boards\n- the 16 full-no-line boards, each with reachability count (number of terminal leaves at that board)\n- confirm every full no-line board is reached in normal play.\n\nI can instrument `rec` to collect terminal boards, but memoized rec doesn't know the board at leaves... Actually I can do a separate DFS (not memoized) collecting terminal boards — 255k paths, fine. Or during rec, return a frozenset of terminal boards per state and union (memoized sets, memory fine). Simpler: separate plain DFS over all paths collecting terminal boards into a set; 255k path traversals ~ 550k nodes, fine.\n\nAlso count moves that end by fill vs by line for standard: X-line 131,184 etc. Already have.\n\nCycle check: I'll add a check that in the plain DFS, every path length ≤ 9 and each move increases stone count by 1 — trivially true; also verify max observed depth across all rules ≤ 9 (assert in analyze: mx <= 9 - start_stones). Actually assert mx <= 9. And print \"DAG: stone count strictly increases; max game length = mx ≤ 9\" — that's the termination proof. I could also run a check on the reachable state graph: count edges where child stone count != parent+1 → should be 0 by construction. Not needed.\n\nOne more thing to verify: rule B's claim \"in 3x3 the last move on a full board is always X\" (standard start). Trivial parity. I can print it.\n\nLet me also compute, for each rule, \"uniform random play\" expected length. And greedy expected length.\n\nFor the \"swap rule\" computation (Part 2 support): For candidate A (and B maybe), compute:\n- no-swap: for each opening c: value({c:X}, O to move)\n- after swap: value({c:O}, X to move)\n- O wins with swap iff for every c, either is O.\nPrint table.\n\nI'll implement a generic `value_of(board, turn, rule)` by making `analyze` expose value; easier: make a module-level `minimax(rule, start, start_turn)` function returning (value, first_move_values).\n\nLet me restructure: `solve(rule, start, start_turn)` returns a dict with everything including per-first-move values; and `report` prints.\n\nAlso the F variants: start = O at 4, turn=X. Rule = std fill wins (F-i) and std fill loses (F-ii). Length = stones - 1.\n\nAlso: report draws count explicitly.\n\nLet me also add rule E with sane interpretation and note min length 3.\n\nAlso Maker-Breaker G. And maybe a rule H = \"standard, fill → the player with more two-in-a-rows wins, else X\"? Not requested. Skip. But maybe add another candidate from literature: \"O wins ties\" = B (already), \"misère with fill last loses\" = D, etc.\n\nNow, the deliverable file: docs/research/no-draw-variants.md. The task says \"Exactly ONE Markdown file in the repo: docs/research/no-draw-variants.md\". Need to create docs/research dir. That's implied by the path. \"Do not create any file other than docs/research/no-draw-variants.md\" — creating the directory is necessary. Fine.\n\nContent outline for the Markdown:\n\n# No-draw variants of 3×3 tic-tac-toe — solver evidence and literature\n- Scope, method summary, reproducibility statement.\n- Table of candidates (comparison table as the user wants).\n- Part 0: Standard baseline (A): 255,168, splits, perfect play draw, terminal position counts, the 16 full no-line boards listed, reachability.\n- Part 1: exhaustive solver results per candidate A–F (+G Maker-Breaker as literature cr