the file.\n\nActually, one more consideration on the greedy policy: the task says \"win now if possible, else block opponent's immediate win, else random\". My `safe` generalization = moves after which opponent has no immediate win. But a literal reading of \"block\" is: if opponent currently threatens to win (has ≥1 immediate winning move in the current position), then choose a move that prevents it; the standard choice is to occupy their winning square; if opponent has multiple winning squares, no single move blocks all. My `safe` approach is equivalent when a block exists and generalizes correctly otherwise. I'll document the interpretation. Also note: greedy \"win now\" check uses the rule's terminal function after the move.\n\nAnother subtlety in greedy: when the mover has an immediate win, they take it (uniform among multiple winning moves). Good.\n\nNow consider the greedy policy under rule E: \"win now\" = move that makes any line full → mover wins. Good. \"Block\" = move after which opponent has no move making a full line. Fine.\n\nUnder G (Maker-Breaker): X \"win now\" = completes X line; O \"win now\" never (O wins only at fill; if O's move fills the board with no X line → rule returns O → greedy counts it as a win now!). At the last move, if board fills with no X line, O's move wins immediately → greedy will take it. Good.\n\nLet me now write the code.\n\nAlso: I want the script to print, for each rule, a compact one-line summary plus detailed block. Let me produce output that's easy to paste (not too gigantic). I'll include length histograms maybe only for A and E; skip for others.\n\nLet me estimate output size: 8 rule configurations × ~15 lines = ~120 lines. Fine.\n\nLet me write.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\nimport sys\nfrom fractions import Fraction\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nfrom itertools import combinations\n\nEMPTY, X, O = 0, 1, 2\nLINES = (...)\ndef other...\n...\nRULES = [\n  (\"A  standard + fill -> last mover wins\", R_std_fill_wins, None),\n  (\"B  standard + fill -> last mover loses\", R_std_fill_loses, None),\n  (\"C  misere + fill -> last mover wins\", R_misere_fill_wins, None),\n  (\"D  misere + fill -> last mover loses\", R_misere_fill_loses, None),\n  (\"E  any filled line wins\", R_any_line, None),\n  (\"F(i) O pre-placed centre + fill -> last mover wins\", R_std_fill_wins, O_CENTRE),\n  (\"F(ii) O pre-placed centre + fill -> last mover loses\", R_std_fill_loses, O_CENTRE),\n  (\"G  Maker-Breaker (X wins line; O wins filled board with no X line)\", R_maker_breaker, None),\n]\n```\n\nFor each, `solve` returns a dict; `report` formats.\n\nThen standard extras section (distinct positions, 16 boards, reachability counts, swap check).\n\nLet me be careful about the `rec` memo for F: start board has a stone, and `other(turn)` for root (turn=X) = O, rule(b, O): standard rule → no line/full → None. Good.\n\nNow — one subtle issue with memoization and rule C/D: `rule(b, last)` where last = other(turn); for F root this is O (the pre-placer) — fine.\n\nLet me write it all.\n\nFor the swap check I need minimax value of arbitrary (board, turn). I'll expose `minimax_first_values(rule, start, turn)`.\n\nLet me write the script now. I'll include everything and print.\n\nAlso for the record: \"verify that no candidate allows a reachable cycle\" — I'll implement an explicit check on the reachable standard graph: for every reachable state (b, turn) and every legal move, the child's stone count = parent's + 1; print a global assertion and max stone count. I'll do it for all rules (in rec: assert child stones = b stones + 1). Actually simpler: in rec, assert sum(nb != EMPTY) == sum(b != EMPTY) + 1. If a bug existed, assertion fires. Plus print max depth ≤ 9. That's a proper termination argument (graded DAG + finite depth).\n\nLet me also print the number of reachable states for the standard tree.\n\nCode for the 16 boards + reachability:\n\n```python\ndef full_no_line_boards():\n    out = []\n    for xs in combinations(range(9), 5):\n        b = [O] * 9\n        for i in xs: b[i] = X\n        b = tuple(b)\n        if not mono(b, X) and not mono(b, O):\n            out.append(b)\n    return out\n```\n\nReachability counts: plain DFS from empty using std stopping rule:\n```python\ndef reach_terminal_boards(rule, start, turn):\n    counts = {}\n    stack = [(start, turn)]\n    # need all paths; recursive\n    def dfs(b, turn):\n        w = rule(b, other(turn))\n        if w is not None:\n            counts[b] = counts.get(b, 0) + 1\n            return\n        for i ...: dfs(child, other(turn))\n    dfs(start, turn)\n    return counts\n```\n255k leaves × recursion — fine (a few seconds maybe; Python recursion depth 9, but function call count = nodes ~ 550k, fine).\n\nDo this for standard (A). Count distinct terminal boards = len(counts). Group:\n- X-line: mono(b,X) and not mono(b,O) — count\n- O-line: mono(b,O)\n- full no line: board_full and no mono.\nAlso note whether any board has both lines (should be 0 as terminal).\n\nThen print the 16 boards as 3 lines of X/O/. plus reach counts (min/max/values). The reach counts: number of terminal sequences ending at each board. Let me print each board and count.\n\nAlso check total = 255,168.\n\nNow let me write the file and run. Then research.\n\nLet me create dir /var/folders/.../opencode/ttt_no_draw.\n\nActually, simpler: put it at /var/folders/.../opencode/ttt_no_draw_solver.py. But there's already ttt/ dir from previous session; avoid confusion, create `ttt_no_draw/`.\n\nLet me write.\n\nAfter running, I'll do the literature searches in parallel batches.\n\nSearch queries:\n1. \"255168 tic-tac-toe games 131184 77904 46080\" — find owner.\n2. OEIS search: \"255168\" site:oeis.org or oeis.org/search?q=255168\n3. \"misere tic-tac-toe\" paper / centre rotation strategy.\n4. \"Notakto\" paper primary source.\n5. \"Maker-Breaker\" 3x3 tic-tac-toe Maker wins Beck.\n6. Zermelo 1913 determinacy paper title.\n7. Gale 1979 hex pie rule swap.\n8. Full-board no-line tic-tac-toe positions 16 (OEIS?).\n\nLet me get going. I'll write the solver first.\n\nLet me now write the actual code text carefully.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nno_draw_ttt.py -- exhaustive solvers for 3x3 tic-tac-toe no-draw rule sets.\n\nstdlib only. Every number printed below comes from enumerating the complete\ngame tree (all legal lines of play, stopping at terminal states). No sampling.\n\nCells are numbered 0..8:\n    0 1 2\n    3 4 5\n    6 7 8\nX moves first (rule F excepted: O is pre-placed on the centre).\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom fractions import Fraction\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nfrom itertools import combinations\n\nEMPTY, X, O = 0, 1, 2\nSYM = {X: \"X\", O: \"O\", None: \"draw\"}\nLINES = ((0, 1, 2), (3, 4, 5), (6, 7, 8),\n         (0, 3, 6), (1, 4, 7), (2, 5, 8),\n         (0, 4, 8), (2, 4, 6))\n\n\ndef other(p):\n    return O if p == X else X\n\n\ndef mono(b, p):\n    return any(b[a] == p and b[c] == p and b[d] == p for a, c, d in LINES)\n\n\ndef any_full_line(b):\n    return any(b[a] != EMPTY and b[c] != EMPTY and b[d] != EMPTY for a, c, d in LINES)\n\n\ndef board_full(b):\n    return all(c != EMPTY for c in b)\n\n\ndef stones(b):\n    return sum(1 for c in b if c != EMPTY)\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Terminal rules.  Each takes (board, last_mover) and returns the winner\n# (X or O) if the position is terminal, else None.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef R_std_fill_wins(b, last):\n    if mono(b, X):\n        return X\n    if mono(b, O):\n        return O\n    return last if board_full(b) else None\n\n\ndef R_std_fill_loses(b, last):\n    if mono(b, X):\n        return X\n    if mono(b, O):\n        return O\n    return other(last) if board_full(b) else None\n\n\ndef R_misere_fill_wins(b, last):\n    if mono(b, last):\n        return other(last)\n    if mono(b, other(last)):          # defensive; unreachable in play\n        return last\n    return last if board_full(b) else None\n\n\ndef R_misere_fill_loses(b, last):\n    if mono(b, last):\n        return other(last)\n    if mono(b, other(last)):          # defensive; unreachable in play\n        return last\n    return other(last) if board_full(b) else None\n\n\ndef R_any_line(b, last):\n    return last if any_full_line(b) else None\n\n\ndef R_maker_breaker(b, last):\n    if mono(b, X):\n        return X\n    return O if board_full(b) else None\n\n\nEMPTY9 = (EMPTY,) * 9\nF_START = (EMPTY, EMPTY, EMPTY, EMPTY, O, EMPTY, EMPTY, EMPTY, EMPTY)\n\n\ndef solve(rule, start=EMPTY9, start_turn=X):\n    \"\"\"Exhaustive analysis of one rule set from a given starting position.\"\"\"\n    s0 = stones(start)\n\n    # (1) complete tree: terminal-leaf counts, lengths, exact uniform-play\n    #     probabilities.  Memo key is (board, turn); the previous mover is\n    #     always other(turn) on every non-root call.\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def rec(b, turn):\n        w = rule(b, other(turn))\n        if w is not None:\n            L = stones(b) - s0\n            assert L <= 9 - s0\n            counts = {X: 0, O: 0, None: 0}\n            counts[w] = 1\n            probs = {X: Fraction(0), O: Fraction(0), None: Fraction(0)}\n            probs[w] = Fraction(1)\n            return counts, L, L, probs, 1\n        legal = [i for i in range(9) if b[i] == EMPTY]\n        m = len(legal)\n        counts = {X: 0, O: 0, None: 0}\n        probs = {X: Fraction(0), O: Fraction(0), None: Fraction(0)}\n        mx = 0\n        slen = 0\n        nstates = 0\n        for i in legal:\n            nb = b[:i] + (turn,) + b[i + 1:]\n            assert stones(nb) == stones(b) + 1\n            c2, m2, s2, p2, n2 = rec(nb, other(turn))\n            for k in counts:\n                counts[k] += c2[k]\n            mx = max(mx, m2)\n            slen += s2\n            nstates += n2\n            for k in probs:\n                probs[k] += p2[k] / m\n        return counts, mx, slen, probs, nstates + 1\n```\n\nWait: at a leaf, if w is None (a draw terminal — only possible for standard A under no tiebreak? For A there's no draw since fill → winner. Actually draws can only occur if the rule returns None at a full board, which never happens in A–G because all have a fill tiebreak or stop earlier. Hmm — for standard rules with NO tiebreak (baseline), the full board with no line is a draw. The task says \"Also solve standard rules (no modification) to confirm perfect-play draw and the 255,168 split.\" So I need a baseline rule with draws: R_std_draw: mono → winner; full → None. That's the standard tic-tac-toe. Then A is standard + fill tiebreak. Good, add R_std_draw as baseline. For the leaf with w=None, counts[None] = 1, probs[None] = 1. My code sets counts[w]=1 where w=None works. Good.\n\n(2) minimax value:\n\n```python\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def value(b, turn):\n        w = rule(b, other(turn))\n        if w is not None:\n            return w\n        vals = set()\n        for i in range(9):\n            if b[i] == EMPTY:\n                nb = b[:i] + (turn,) + b[i + 1:]\n                vals.add(value(nb, other(turn)))\n        if turn in vals:\n            return turn\n        if None in vals:\n            return None\n        return other(turn)\n\n    first_values = {}\n    for i in range(9):\n        if start[i] == EMPTY:\n            nb = start[:i] + (start_turn,) + start[i + 1:]\n            first_values[i] = value(nb, other(start_turn))\n```\n\n(3) greedy policy:\n\n```python\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def greedy(b, turn):\n        w = rule(b, other(turn))\n        if w is not None:\n            L = stones(b) - s0\n            counts = {X: 0, O: 0, None: 0}\n            counts[w] = 1\n            probs = {X: Fraction(0), O: Fraction(0), None: Fraction(0)}\n            probs[w] = Fraction(1)\n            return counts, L, L, probs\n        legal = [i for i in range(9) if b[i] == EMPTY]\n        def play(bb, i, p):\n            return bb[:i] + (p,) + bb[i + 1:]\n        wins = [i for i in legal if rule(play(b, i, turn), turn) == turn]\n        if wins:\n            cand = wins\n        else:\n            safe = []\n            for i in legal:\n                nb = play(b, i, turn)\n                if rule(nb, turn) is not None:\n                    continue          # move ends the game without winning\n                opp = other(turn)\n                threat = any(\n                    nb[j] == EMPTY and rule(play(nb, j, opp), opp) == opp\n                    for j in range(9)\n                )\n                if not threat:\n                    safe.append(i)\n            cand = safe if safe else legal\n        m = len(cand)\n        counts = {X: 0, O: 0, None: 0}\n        probs = {X: Fraction(0), O: Fraction(0), None: Fraction(0)}\n        exp = Fraction(0)\n        mx = 0\n        for i in cand:\n            nb = play(b, i, turn)\n            c2, m2, e2, p2 = greedy(nb, other(turn))\n            for k in counts:\n                counts[k] += c2[k]\n            for k in probs:\n                probs[k] += p2[k] / m\n            exp += e2 / m\n            mx = max(mx, m2)\n        return counts, mx, exp, probs\n```\n\nNote leaf return order: (counts, mx, exp, probs). Internal returns (counts, mx, exp, probs). Good.\n\nThen assemble results:\n\n```python\n    counts, mx, slen, probs, nstates = rec(start, start_turn)\n    total_leaves = sum(counts.values())\n    gcounts, gmx, gexp, gprobs = greedy(start, start_turn)\n    return {\n        \"counts\": counts, \"total\": total_leaves, \"max_len\": mx,\n        \"mean_len\": Fraction(slen, total_leaves),\n        \"prob\": probs, \"states\": nstates,\n        \"value\": value(start, start_turn),\n        \"first_values\": first_values,\n        \"winning_first\": [i for i, v in first_values.items() if v == start_turn],\n        \"greedy_counts\": gcounts, \"greedy_max\": gmx,