            "output": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_654ae4255cb0f7c6b7c91011fc4033a1\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1301.1672v1\",\n      \"title\": \"The Secrets of Notakto: Winning at X-only Tic-Tac-Toe\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-11-27\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The Secrets of Notakto: Winning at X-only Tic-Tac-Toe\\nThane E. Plambeck, Greg Whitehead November 27, 2024 Abstract We analyze misere play of “impartial” tic-tac-toe—a game suggested by Bob Koca in which both players make X’s on the board, and the first player to complete three-in-a-row loses . This game was recently discussed on mathoverflow.net in a thread created by Timothy Y. Chow.\\n1 Introduction\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1301.1672\",\n      \"title\": \"The Secrets of Notakto: Winning at X-only Tic-Tac-Toe\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"aac 2\\nthat move on the other board, we’d lose rather than win! For example, from\\nthe N-position shown in Figure 5, there certainly is a winning move, but it’s not\\nto the upper-right-hand corner of the board on the right, which loses.\\nFigure 4:\\nAn N-position in which mimickry loses.\\nWe invite our reader to find a correct reply!\\n6\\naac 2\\nThe iPad game Notakto\\nEvidently the computation of general outcomes in misere tic-tac-toe is somewhat\\ncomplicated, involving computations in finite monoid and looking up values from\\n4\\na table of all possible single-board positions.\\nHowever, we’ve found that a human can develop the ability to win from\\naac 2\\nmulti-board positions with some practice.\\nFigure 5:\\nA six-board game of Notakto, in progress.\\nNotakto “No tac toe” is an iPad game that allows the user to practice\\nplaying misere X-only Tic-Tac-Toe against a computer. Impartial misere tic-\\ntac-toe from start positions involving one up to as many as six initial tic-tac-toe\\naac 2\\nboards are supported.\\nThe Notakto iPad application is available for free at http://www.notakto.com .\\n7\\nFinal question\\nDoes the 4x4 game have a finite misere quotient?\\n5\\nReferences\\n[Chow]\\nTimothy Y. Chow, Online mathoverflow.net question, “Neutral tic\\ntac toe”, 16 May 2010.\\nhttp://mathoverflow.net/questions/24693/neutral-tic-tac-toe/\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.mathgametime.com/games/notakto\",\n      \"title\": \"Notakto – Math Game Time\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"* HOME\\n* MATH GAMES\\n* MATH VIDEOS\\n* MATH WORKSHEETS\\n* MOBILE GAMES\\n* BLOG\\nNotakto\\nThink tic tac toe, but different. In this free logic game, the goal is to avoid getting three X's in a straight line. The first to do so loses.\\nCopyright © 2026 Fila, LLC\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tic-tac-toe_variants\",\n      \"title\": \"Tic-tac-toe variants - Wikipedia\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2016-12-02\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Notakto\\nNotakto is a misere and impartial form of tic tac toe. This means unlike in misere tic tac toe, in Notakto, both players play as the same symbol, X. [ 18 ] It also can be played on one or multiple boards. [ 19 ]\\nVariants with bigger boards\\nQuixo\\nIn wild tic-tac-toe , players can choose to place either an X or O on each move. [ 7 ] [ 39 ] [ 40 ] [ 41 ] It can be played as a normal game where the player who makes three in a row wins or a misere game where they would lose. [ 7 ] This game is also called your-choice tic-tac-toe [ 42 ] or Devil's tic-tac-toe. [ citation needed ]\\nSOS\\nA completed game of Treblecross\\nIn Treblecross , both players play with the same symbol (an X [ 15 ] or black chip [ 45 ] ). The game is played on a 1-by- n board with k equal to 3. [ 15 ] The player who makes a three in a row of Xs (or black chips) wins the game. [ 15 ] [ 45 ]\\nRevenge n -in-a-row\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://wikimili.com/en/Notakto\",\n      \"title\": \"Notakto - WikiMili, The Best Wikipedia Reader\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"* A completed game of Notakto played across three boards\\n* Genres | paper-and-pencil game\\n* Playing time | 1-15 minutes\\nNotakto is a tic-tac-toe variant , also known as neutral or impartial tic-tac-toe .\\n[1] [2] The game is a combination of the games tic-tac-toe and Nim , [1] [3] played across one or several boards with both of the players playing the same piece (an \\\"X\\\" or cross). The game ends when all the boards contain a three-in-a-row of Xs, [4] [5] [6] at which point the player to have made the last move loses the game.\\nNotakto is played on a finite number of empty three-by-three boards. [4] Then, each player takes turns placing an X on the board(s) in a vacant space (a space not occupied by an X already on the board). [9] If a board has a three-in-a-row, the board is dead and it cannot be played on any more.\\n[2] [7] When one player makes a three-in-a-row and there are no more boards to play on, that player loses. [2] [9]\\nThe \\\"finger trap\\\", in which the first player (who started by playing the center \\\"X\\\") is guaranteed a win Notakto boot trap.svg The \\\"finger trap\\\", in which the first player (who started by playing the center \\\"X\\\") is guaranteed a win\\nThe optimal strategy for a single-board game of Notakto allows the first player to force a win.\\nWith two boards, the second player should on their first move play in the center square of the empty board (the one with no Xs in it). Then, the second player sacrifices one of the boards (by making a three-in-a-row) if it is possible.\\nNow, the game is a 1-board game of Notakto so the second player uses the knight's move or boot trap strategies to win. [9]\\nFrom these two strategies, any game with more than two boards can always be won by the first player (on an odd number of boards) or by the second player (on an even number of boards). [9]\\nTic-tac-toe Paper-and-pencil game for two players\\nTic-tac-toe , noughts and crosses , or Xs and Os is a paper-and-pencil game for two players who take turns marking the spaces in a three-by-three grid with X or O . The player who succeeds in placing three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal row is the winner.\\nThe first player to achieve four of their own markers in a row wins. The winning row can be horizontal, vertical, or diagonal on a single board as in regular tic-tac-toe, or vertically in a column, or a diagonal line through four boards.\\nWild tic-tac-toe\\nWild tic-tac-toe is an impartial game similar to tic-tac-toe. However, in this game players can choose to place either X or O on each move. This game can also be played in its misere form where if a player creates a three-in-a-row of marks, that player loses the game.\\nTic-tac-toe variants Overview about tic-tac-toe variants\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://playpendium.com/notakto\",\n      \"title\": \"Notakto - Play Free Online | PlayPendium\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Notakto\\nA devilish twist on noughts and crosses: both players mark X, and completing a line loses you the game.\\nWas this fun?\\nHow to play\\nTake turns placing an X on any open cell. Unlike normal tic-tac-toe, there are no Os and no winner by line. Whoever is forced to complete a three-in-a-row loses that board, and on a single board that means the game.\\n* Every mark is an X, shared by both players\\n* Completing three in a row kills that board\\n* On multi-board games, play continues on the surviving boards\\n* Avoid being the one who fills the final line Controls\\n* Desktop: click an empty cell to place your X\\n* Phone & tablet: tap an empty cell Game modes\\n* One-board or three-board variants\\n* Versus CPU on Easy, Medium or Hard\\n* Two-player hotseat on one device, plus Online play\\n* Choose who moves first against the computer Tips & strategy\\n* Think defensively: every move you make could be the trap that ends you\\n* On three boards, sacrifice a board deliberately to control the parity\\n* Force your opponent into positions where any move completes a line About Notakto\\nNotakto is an original free browser take on misère noughts-and-crosses, a combinatorial strategy puzzle where the goal is to make your opponent complete the line. Play instantly, no sign-up.\\nA free, original browser game on PlayPendium. No download, no install.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://infosekolah.net/en/Notakto\",\n      \"title\": \"Notakto\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"[ 1 ] [ 2 ] The game is a combination of the games tic-tac-toe and Nim , [ 1 ] [ 3 ] played across one or several boards with both of the players playing the same piece (an \\\"X\\\" or cross). The game ends when all the boards contain a three-in-a-row of Xs, [ 4 ] [ 5 ] [ 6 ] at which point the player to have made the last move loses the game.\\nNotakto is played on a finite number of empty three-by-three boards. [ 4 ] Then, each player takes turns placing an X on the board(s) in a vacant space (a space not occupied by an X already on the board). [ 9 ] If a board has a three-in-a-row, the board is dead and it cannot be played on any more.\\n[ 2 ] [ 7 ] When one player makes a three-in-a-row and there are no more boards to play on, that player loses. [ 2 ] [ 9 ]\\nThe \\\"finger trap\\\", in which the first player (who started by playing the center \\\"X\\\") is guaranteed a win\\nThe optimal strategy for a single-board game of Notakto allows the first player to force a win.\\nIt is for the first player to play the center and then play a knight's move (two squares vertically and one square horizontally, or vice versa) away from the opponent's play. [ 9 ] This strategy works because it makes a boot-like structure, which is called the boot trap. From the boot trap position the first player will be able to force a win.\\n[ 9 ]\\nWith two boards, the second player should on their first move play in the center square of the empty board (the one with no Xs in it). Then, the second player sacrifices one of the boards (by making a three-in-a-row) if it is possible.\\nNow, the game is a 1-board game of Notakto so the second player uses the knight's move or boot trap strategies to win. [ 9 ]\\nFrom these two strategies, any game with more than two boards can always be won by the first player (on an odd number of boards) or by the second player (on an even number of boards). [ 9 ]\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://markhuckvale.com/games/notakto\",\n      \"title\": \"Notakto Game\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Notakto Game\\nNotakto is just like TicTacToe, but on three boards, and both players play X, and the person that completes three in a row is the loser.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://kiwi.itspektar.net/content/wikipedia_en_all_maxi/A/Notakto\",\n      \"title\": \"Notakto\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Notakto is a tic-tac-toe variant , also known as neutral or impartial tic-tac-toe . [1] [2] The game is a combination of the games tic-tac-toe and  , [1] [3] played across one or several boards with both of the players playing the same piece (an \\\"X\\\" or cross).\\nThe game ends when all the boards contain a three-in-a-row of Xs, [4] [5] [6] at which point the player to have made the last move loses the game. [7] However, in this game, unlike tic-tac-toe, there will always be a player who wins any game of Notakto. [8]\\n* A completed game of Notakto played across three boards\\n* Genres | paper-and-pencil game\\n* Playing time | 1-15 minutes\\nNotakto is an impartial game , where the allowable moves depend only on the state of the game and not on which player is taking their turn. When played across multiple boards it is a disjunctive game .\\nNotakto is played on a finite number of empty three-by-three boards. [4] Then, each player takes turns placing an X on the board(s) in a vacant space (a space not occupied by an X already on the board). [9] If a board has a three-in-a-row, the board is dead and it cannot be played on any more.\\n[2] [7] When one player makes a three-in-a-row and there are no more boards to play on, that player loses. [2] [9] ## Optimal strategy\\nThe \\\"boot trap\\\", in which the first player (who started by playing the center \\\"X\\\") is guaranteed a win\\nThe optimal strategy for a single-board game of Notakto allows the first player to force a win.\\nIt is for the first player to play the center and then play a knight's move (two squares vertically and one square horizontally, or vice versa) away from the opponent's play. [9] This strategy works because it makes a boot-like structure, which is called the boot trap. From the boot trap position the first player will be able to force a win. [9]\\nWith two boards, the second player should on their first move play in the center square of the empty board (the one with no Xs in it). Then, the second player sacrifices one of the boards (by making a three-in-a-row) if it is possible.\\nNow, the game is a 1-board game of Notakto so the second player uses the knight's move or boot trap strategies to win. [9]\\nFrom these two strategies, any game with more than two boards can always be won by the first player (on an odd number of boards) or by the second player (on an even number of boards). [9] ## See also\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.wikiland.org/wiki/Tic-tac-toe_variants\",\n      \"title\": \"Tic-tac-toe variants | Wikiland\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Découvrez Tic-tac-toe variants : définition, histoire, contexte et informations essentielles sur Wikiland.\\nPage semi-protected\\nA complete game of Notakto, a misère variant of the game\\nTic-tac-toe is an instance of an m,n,k-game , where two players alternate taking turns on an m × n board until one of them gets k in a row.\\nNotakto\\nNotakto is a misere and impartial form of tic tac toe. This means unlike in misere tic tac toe, in Notakto, both players play as the same symbol, X. [ 18 ] It also can be played on one or multiple boards. [ 19 ]\\nVariants with bigger boards\\nQuixo\\nIn wild tic-tac-toe , players can choose to place either an X or O on each move. [ 7 ] [ 39 ] [ 40 ] [ 41 ] It can be played as a normal game where the player who makes three in a row wins or a misere game where they would lose. [ 7 ] This game is also called your-choice tic-tac-toe [ 42 ] or Devil's tic-tac-toe.\\nSOS\\nA completed game of Treblecross\\nIn Treblecross , both players play with the same symbol (an X [ 15 ] or black chip [ 45 ] ). The game is played on a 1-by- n board with k equal to 3. [ 15 ] The player who makes a three in a row of Xs (or black chips) wins the game. [ 15 ] [ 45 ]\\nRevenge n -in-a-row\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_f3d77f36dffecafp5INUqno9Jy\"\n}",
