            "output": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_372fc048a08446a1c057a960059d2cb9\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2007.15141v3\",\n      \"title\": \"Pairing strategies for the Maker–Breaker game on the hypercube with subcubes as winning sets\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Abstract\\nWe consider the Maker–Breaker positional game on the vertices of the n-dimensional hypercube {0, 1} n with k-dimensional subcubes as winning sets. We describe a pairing strategy which allows Breaker to win if n is a power of 4 and k ≥ n/4 + 1. Our results also imply that for all n ≥ 3 there is a Breaker’s win pairing strategy if k ≥  3\\nA positional game can be thought of as a generalization of Tic-Tac-Toe played on a hyper- graph (V, H) where the vertices can be considered the “board” on which the game is played, and the hyperedges can be considered the “winning sets.” More formally, a positional game on (V, H) is a two-player game where at every turn each player alternately\\nNormal 3 × 3 Tic-Tac-Toe is a strong positional game where the vertices of the hypergraph are the nine positions and the hyperedges are the eight winning lines. In a Maker–Breaker positional game, the first player, Maker, wins if Maker occupies all vertices of some hyperedge A ∈ H, otherwise the second player, Breaker, wins.\\nTherefore, by definition there are no draw plays in Maker–Breaker games. We say that a player P has a winning strategy if no matter how the other player plays, player P wins by following that strategy. It is well-known that in a finite Maker–Breaker game, exactly one player has a winning strategy.\\nUsing a general Breaker’s win criterion (Erd˝os–Selfridge theorem [16]) and Maker’s win criterion (Theorem 1.2 [5]), they showed that Breaker has a winning strategy for the Q(n, k) game when k ≥ log2(n) + 1 and Maker has a winning strategy when k ≤ log2 log2(n) − 1.\\n18 (2006), pp. 2212–2218.\\n[4] J. Balogh, P. Hu, B. Lidick´y, and H. Liu, Upper bounds on the size of 4- and 6-cycle-free subgraphs of the hypercube, European J. Combin., 35 (2014), 75–85.\\n[5] J. Beck, Combinatorial Games: Tic-Tac-Toe Theory, Cambridge University Press, 2008.\\n[6] J.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://math.colgate.edu/~dmhoward/ThesisPaper.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"A Study of Combinatorial Games\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"For example, Tic-Tac-Toe under the normal rules optimally will end\\nin a draw. Suppose you alter those rules ever so slightly and say that a win for player two\\nis if they stop player one from obtaining a winning line (This is called the Maker-Breaker\\nversion of a positional game). In this case player one has a winning strategy.\\nWe first examine the game of Tic-Tac-Toe\\nas an example. The traditional game, as everybody knows, is played on a 2-dimensional on\\na 3x3 board. However we can extend this game to any dimension and any size n and say\\nthat a winner is anyone who obtains a winning set. We call this the n d -game. A winning\\nii.) player one is trying to obtain a winning set from F and the other is\\ntrying to stop him. (Maker-Breaker game)\\nIn positional games, it often helps to alter the rules slightly to give us more of an understand-\\ning of the game. Thus we create the Maker and Breaker version of a positional game. We\\ncan see that in the traditional positional game of tic-tac-toe, each player is trying to occupy\\na winning position before the other. Now instead of players trying to occupy a winning set,\\nwe look at this game as Maker trying to occupy a winning set, with Breaker trying to stop\\nthem. Clearly, there is no way to draw in this game.\\nYou can see that in the ordinary tic-tac-toe game (3 2 -game) there is no win in the 3 2 game.\\nHowever, this game has a weak-win. If you look at the Maker-Breaker version a winning\\nstrategy is available for player one. This is shown in Figure 1. The moves for each player are\\ndenoted by the subscripts.\\nThus if Breaker has a winning set on one ( F or F ´)\\nthen Maker has a win on the other half set since F and F ´ are copies and Maker has the\\ncopy of Breaker’s winning set in the opposite half.\\n□\\nThe most common way to guarantee a win or draw in a game is through a pairing strategy. In\\nother words whenever a Player makes a move you have a counter move that somehow negates\\nthe other Players benefits of his previous move. We showed a couple pairing strategies above:\\nthe pairing strategy one can give to obtain a weak win in the Maker and Breaker version\\nof 3 2 game (Tic-Tac-Toe).\\n(2 2 and the 3 3 of the n d game)\\n2.) There exists a draw ending position but still Player one has a winning strategy (4 3 game)\\n3.) The symmetric game has a drawing strategy but there exists a weak-win (3 2 ordinary\\ntic-tac-toe)\\n4.) There is a strong drawing strategy for Player two in the symmetric game (4 2 ) game\\n[2] Joz´ef Becks Notes on Combinatorial Games (unpublished), Jozef Beck\\n[3] The One-Round Voronoi Game, Otfried Cheong, Sariel Har-Peled,Nathan Linial, Jir ´ i\\nMatousek, 2002\\n[4] Competitive facility Location:The Voronoi Game, Hee-Kap Ahn; Siu-Wing Cheng; Ot-\\nfried Cheong; Mordecai Golin; Ren´e van Oostrum, 2003\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2209.12819\",\n      \"title\": \"[PDF] Maker-Breaker is solved in polynomial time on hypergraphs of rank 3\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Abstract\\nIn the Maker-Breaker positional game, Maker and Breaker take turns picking vertices of a hypergraph H , and Maker wins if and only if she possesses all the vertices of some edge of H . Deciding the outcome (i.e., which player has a winning strategy) is a PSPACE-complete problem even when restricted to 4-uniform hypergraphs [Gal25].\\nAnother corollary of our structural result is that, if Maker has a winning strategy on a hypergraph of rank 3, then she can ensure to win the game within a number of rounds that is logarithmic in the number of vertices.\\nThe first general formulation of this convention goes back to Hales and Jewett [HJ63], while the first general results are due to Erdős and Selfridge [ES73]. The game of tic-tac-toe and its generalizations [HJ63, Bec08] are the most famous examples of Maker-Maker positional games.\\n1\\nMaker wins if and only if, at some point during the game, there is a fully marked edge, i.e., an edge whose vertices are all marked.\\n5\\nAn example on the \\\"tic-tac-toe hypergraph\\\" is given in Figure 1: here, we see that Maker wins by completing the middle row of the hypergraph.\\nAs an illustration of how the family D1 helps us understand the Maker-Breaker game, consider the tic-tac-toe hypergraph H (Figure 1, left). It can easily be shown that H is a Maker win, and that the center vertex or any corner vertex is a winning first move for Maker.\\nFigure 10: Four x -cycles in the tic-tac-toe hypergraph, where x is the top-left vertex.\\nContrary to the previous example (tic-tac-toe hypergraph), considering only the D1-dangers did not allow Breaker to predict his loss before the game began: only after the first round did he realize his fate. How could Breaker have foreseen the threat?\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cs.tau.ac.il/~krivelev/fastgame.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast winning strategies in Maker-Breaker games\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Beck also notes that his result is essentially tight: playing on the 3-chromatic almost disjoint n-uniform hypergraph constructed by Erd˝os and Lov´asz [8] on n 4 2 n vertices, Maker wins and consequently does so in at most n 4 2 n − 1 moves.\\n[1] J. Beck, On positional games, J. of Combinatorial Theory, Ser. A 30 (1981) 117– 133.\\n[2] J. Beck, Remarks on positional games, Acta Math. Acad. Sci. Hungar. 40 (1-2) (1982) 65–71.\\n[3] J. Beck, Combinatorial Games: Tic-Tac-Toe Theory, Cambridge University Press, 2008.\\n[4] M. Bednarska and T.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maker-Breaker_game\",\n      \"title\": \"Maker-Breaker game\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Maker-Breaker game\\nIn a Maker-Breaker game, Maker wins if he manages to hold all the elements of a winning-set, while Breaker wins if he manages to prevent this, i.e. to hold at\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.14256\",\n      \"title\": \"4 PSPACE Maker-Maker games of rank are -complete M\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"A well-known strategy-stealing argument [HJ63] shows that optimal play leads to one of two possible outcomes: a first player win or a draw. The most famous example of a Maker-Maker game is the tic-tac-toe game on a 3 × 3 grid, which can be generalized into the k-in-a-row game on grids of any size[Bec08].\\n[Bec08] J. Beck. Combinatorial Games: Tic-Tac-Toe Theory. Academic Press, Cambridge, 2008. ISBN 978-0-521-46100-9.\\n[Bys04] J. M. Byskov. Maker-Maker and Maker-Breaker games are PSPACE-complete. BRICS Report Series, 11(14), 2004. http://dx.doi.org/10.7146/brics.v11i14.21839.\\n[CE78] V. Chvátal, P. Erdős. Biased positional games. Ann. Disc.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cs.umd.edu/~gasarch/COURSES/752/S22/Combgamesttt.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"[PDF] Combinatorial Games: Tic-Tac-Toe Theory - UMD Computer Science\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"[PDF] Combinatorial Games: Tic-Tac-Toe Theory - UMD Computer Science\\nIn this comprehensive volume, József Beck shows readers how to escape from the combinatorial chaos via the fake probabilistic method, a game-theoretic\\n > \\nCombinatorial Games: Tic-Tac-Toe Theory\\nThe objective of this book is exactly to show an escape from Combinatorial Chaos, to win a battle in a hopeless war. This “victory” on the class of Tic-Tac\\n > \\n[PDF] Combinatorial Games: Tic-Tac-Toe Theory - UMD Computer Science\\nThis is the subject of Combinatorial Game Theory. Most board games are a challenge for mathematics: to analyze a position one has to examine the available\\n > \\n[PDF] Combinatorial Games: Tic-Tac-Toe Theory - UMD Computer Science\\nCombinatorial Game means a 2-player zero- sum game of skill (no chance moves) with complete information, and the payoff function has 3 values only: win, draw,\\n > \\nCombinatorial Games: Tic-Tac-Toe Theory\\nIn this comprehensive volume, József Beck shows readers how to escape from the combinatorial chaos via the fake probabilistic method, a game-theoretic\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1605.05430\",\n      \"title\": \"arXiv:1605.05430v1  [math.CO]  18 May 2016\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"In the Maker-Maker version of the game, the player who is first to take all elements of some edge A ∈ E wins the game. There are a number of beautiful and difficult theorems about Maker-Maker games, for more details Berlekamp, Conway and Guy: [7] or Beck: [5] or [6].\\nCombinatorics, Probability and Computing 14 (2005), 649–696. [6] J. Beck, Combinatorial games. Tic-Tac-Toe Theory. Encyclopedia of Mathematics and its Ap- plications, 114. Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 2008. xiv+732 pp. [7] E. R. Berlekamp, J. H. Conway and R. K. Guy, Winning Ways for your mathematical plays.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combinatorial_Games%3A_Tic-Tac-Toe_Theory\",\n      \"title\": \"Combinatorial Games: Tic-Tac-Toe Theory\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Combinatorial Games: Tic-Tac-Toe Theory\\nCombinatorial Games: Tic-Tac-Toe Theory is a monograph on the mathematics of tic-tac-toe and other positional games, written by József Beck.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v32i4p41/pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Solving Maker-Breaker Games on 5-Uniform Hypergraphs is PSPACE-Complete\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Let X be a finite vertex set and F ⊆ P ( X ). Then, ( X, F ) is a hypergraph and max F ∈F | F | is its rank . Let t ∈ { m , b } . The Maker-Breaker game ( X, F , t ) is a combinatorial game between two players, Maker and Breaker. In the context of a Maker-Breaker game, we call the elements of X squares and the elements of F winning combinations .\\nThis means that Breaker will lose unless he claims p . Similarly, if F contains two combinations { p, q } and { p, r } where p , q , and r are pairwise distinct, we say that in ( X, F , b), Maker is threatening mate in two . Here, Breaker will lose unless he claims p , q or r .\\nExample 2. Consider the Maker-Breaker version of Tic-Tac-Toe.\\nThe position after Maker claims 1, Breaker responds with 5, and Maker claims 9 reduces to the Maker-Breaker game ( { 1 , 2 , 4 , 6 , 7 , 8 } , {{ 2 , 3 } , { 7 , 8 } , { 4 , 7 } , { 3 , 6 }} , b).\\nM 2 3\\n4 B 6\\n7 8 M\\nIn that game, Maker is threatening mate in two in two places at the same time: 2 , 3 , 6 and 4 , 7 , 8.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_f3d77f36dffecafp5INUqno9Jy\"\n}",
