            "output": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_0a615520f2a5688b84ee3a7e61c06387\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.ams.org/notices/202108/rnoti-p1345.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Hex: The Full Story\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The second is that the first player should win. For\\nthis reason, going first is possibly unfair. Normally pieces\\ncannot be moved but one important exception used by\\nmost players is called the swap rule . On their first move\\nthe second player may move normally, or choose to swap\\ntheir piece with that placed by the first player. This encour-\\nages the first player to only choose a moderately strong first\\nmove and so reduces any advantage of going first. For new\\nplayers the swap rule is confusing, and it is best to ignore\\nthe swap rule for the first few games. If you have not played\\nHex, then now would be a good time to do so.\\nOriginally the game was played on disposable pads,\\nwith\\neach\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pie_rule\",\n      \"title\": \"Pie rule - Wikipedia\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-01-30\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Player 1 to play again, now as Black\\nThe pie rule , sometimes referred to as the swap rule , is a rule used to balance abstract strategy games where a first-move advantage has been demonstrated. After the first move is made in a game that uses the pie rule, the second player must select one of two options:\\n1. Letting the move stand. The second player remains the second player and moves immediately.\\n2. Switching places. The second player becomes the first-moving player with the move already done by the opponent, and the opponent plays the first move of their new color.\\nDepending on the game, there may be two ways to implement switching places.\\n3.\\nSwitching colors means that the players exchange pieces. The player who made the first move becomes the second player and makes the second move on the board. This is demonstrated in the chess diagrams shown here.\\n4. Switching the first piece can occur in games where the board starts empty and the first move consists of placing one piece.\\nSuppose the colors are white versus black, and black places the first piece. This piece is replaced by a white piece in the corresponding location for white , and the black piece is returned to black's supply. In a game such as Hex or TwixT , the corresponding location is at a cell \\\"reflected\\\" across the nearest (or either) diagonal.\\nIn games such as Y , where the board is not directional, the white stone replaces the black stone in the same cell. Players keep their respective color pieces, and play continues with black making the next move. This is effectively the same as switching colors.\\nThe use of pie rule was first reported in 1909 for a game in the Mancala family.\\n[ 1 ] Among modern games, Hex uses this rule. [ 2 ] TwixT in tournament play uses a swap rule. [ 3 ] In Meridians , the first player places 2 stones on the board before the second player chooses the color. The rule can be applied to other games which are otherwise solved for one player, such as Gomoku or Tablut . [ 4 ]\\nThe rule gets its name from the divide and choose method of ensuring fairness in when dividing a pie between two people: one person cuts the pie in half, then the other person chooses which half to eat. The person cutting the pie, knowing that the other person will choose the larger piece, will make as equal a division as possible.\\nThis rule acts as a normalization factor in games where there may be a first-move advantage.\\nIn games that cannot end in a draw, such as Hex, the pie rule theoretically gives the second player a win (since one of the players must have a winning strategy after the first move, and the second player can choose to be this player), but the practical result is that the first player will choose a move neither too strong nor too weak, and the\\nsecond player will have to decide whether switching places is worth the first-move advantage.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://hexagain.com/articles/rules\",\n      \"title\": \"Hexagain\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"As in most abstract strategy games, the first player has a very strong advantage in Hex.\\nTo mitigate this, it’s common for one player to place the first black stone, and for the other player to decide who plays black.\\nIf the opening move is too strong, then the second player will decide to play as black.\\nIf the opening move is too weak, then the player who made it is stuck with it while their opponent makes a good move as white.\\nThis creates a natural motivation to balance the first move as much as possible.\\nThe term “pie rule” comes from slicing a pie in half. One person makes a cut, the other.\\nThe person making the cut knows they’re going to get the smaller piece of pie, so they try to make the pieces as equally-sized as possible.\\nThis is also called the “swap rule.”\\nTwo players of different skill levels can still have interesting games by assigning a handicap to the stronger player.\\nThe simplest handicap is simply playing without the pie rule, and letting the weaker player move first.\\nThe weaker player can be given a further advantage by letting them start with pieces on the board.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://hex-game-webapp-825238191980.us-central1.run.app/rules\",\n      \"title\": \"Play Game\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Swap 'Pie' Rule\\nThe swap rule states that after red plays the first move, blue decides whether to swap colours or not. If blue swaps colours, blue becomes red and red becomes blue. Whichever player ends up being blue makes the second move and then the game continues as usual.\\nWhen playing Hex without the swap rule, the first player has a considerable advantage.\\nThe swap rule was devised to make the game more even. Namely, if the first player plays a move that is too strong, the second player will swap and be in a strong position. And if the first player plays a move that is too weak, the second player will not swap (and therefore also be in a strong position).\\nTherefore, the swap rule creates an incentive for the first player to play a move that is as fair as possible.\\nThe swap rule is sometimes also called the \\\"pie rule\\\", since it resembles the well-known \\\"you cut, I choose\\\" method for fairly dividing a pie between two people.\\nNamely, one person cuts the pie in two, and the other person chooses which piece to eat. Here, the incentive for the first person is to make the two pieces as equal as possible.\\nSince each opening move is theoretically either winning or losing, there exists no opening move that is exactly fair.\\nFor this reason, the second player theoretically has a forced win when playing with the swap rule. However, the second player's advantage is very small, and certainly much smaller than the first player's advantage would be when playing without the swap rule.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://boardgamegeek.com/thread/513596/the-pie-rule-great-or-sucky\",\n      \"title\": \"The pie rule: Great, or sucky?\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2010-04-07\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The pie rule: Great, or sucky?\\nApr 7, 2010 — I first pie rule means that one player makes their first move ; the second player then can either move as normal, or can switch places with the\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cut-the-knot.org/Curriculum/Games/HexTheory.shtml\",\n      \"title\": \"Hex Can't End in a Draw\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"To make this work, the first player makes a very first move and, in a sense, forgets about it. He imagines now being a second player and, as such, playing the winning strategy on the board with an extra chip of his color. Note that the extra chip can do no harm to this player.\\nThus in the game of Hex, the first player has an advantage. Different stratagems have been devised to minimize this advantage [ Gardner , Browne ]. However, it must be said, that the game is sufficiently complicated for a novice to make use of this advantage. A good second player will have no difficulty to consistently thrush a game opening novice.\\n* What if applet does not run?\\nDiscussion\\n|Contact| |Front page| |Contents| |Interactive activities|\\nCopyright © 1996-2018 Alexander Bogomolny\\nLet's then prove that Hex can't end in a draw. The result is not trivial. David Gale has even shown that the fact that Hex can't end in a draw is equivalent to Brouwer's Fixed Point theorem.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://sites.google.com/site/boardandpieces/terminology/pie-rule\",\n      \"title\": \"Board and Pieces - Pie Rule\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Board and Pieces - Pie Rule\\nAlso known as the swap rule, the pie rule works to take the first player advantage away and equalize fairness for both players. Using the pie rule,\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hex_%28board_game%29\",\n      \"title\": \"Hex (board game) - Wikipedia\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2026-07-31\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"* Connection game\\n* Players | 2\\n* Setup time | None\\n* Playing time | 30 minutes – 2 hours (11×11 board)\\n* Chance | None\\n* Skills | Strategy , tactics\\nHex (also called Nash ) is a two-player abstract strategy board game in which players attempt to connect opposite sides of a rhombus-shaped board made of hexagonal cells .\\nEach player's goal is to form a connected path of their own stones linking their two board edges. The player who completes such a connection wins the game.\\nTo compensate for the first player's advantage, the swap rule (also called the pie rule) is normally used.\\nThis rule allows the second player to choose whether to switch positions with the first player after the first player makes the first move.\\nWhen it is clear to both players who will win the game, it is customary, but not required, for the losing player to resign. In practice, most games of Hex end with one of the players resigning.\\nIn 1979, David Gale published a proof that the determinacy of Hex is equivalent to the two-dimensional Brouwer fixed-point theorem , and that the determinacy of higher-dimensional n -player variants proves the fixed-point theorem in general. [ 26 ]\\n[ edit ]\\nIn Hex without the swap rule on any board of size n x n , the first player has a theoretical winning strategy.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://videogamegeek.com/thread/2109114/on-the-pie-rule-and-its-use-to-balance-2-player-ab/page/3\",\n      \"title\": \"On the pie rule and its use to balance 2-player abstract ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2018-12-06\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"On the pie rule and its use to balance 2-player abstract ...\\nDec 6, 2018 — the first player wins after opening with F6 , which is clearly the strongest move. the size of the game tree significantly. the pie rule does\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://danielsolisblog.blogspot.com/2013/09/the-pie-rule-is-delicious.html\",\n      \"title\": \"The Pie Rule is Delicious\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2013-09-05\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The Pie Rule is Delicious\\nSep 5, 2013 — The Pie Rule is a method of balancing the first-player advantage present in many abstract games. If I'm the first player, I take my first turn.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_f3d77f36dffecafp5INUqno9Jy\"\n}",
