            "output": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_772c4779bb397ff252bb27dc5a56796e\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www1.cmc.edu/pages/faculty/MONeill/math188/papers/Gale1.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem\\nby D GALE · 1979 · Cited by 392 — The general Hex Theorem and fixed-point algorithm are presented in the final section. We can now state for the third time HEX THEOREM. variants\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/00029890.1979.11994922\",\n      \"title\": \"The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem\\nby D Gale · 1979 · Cited by 399 — (1979). The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem. The American Mathematical Monthly: Vol. 86, No. 10, pp. 818-827.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/pdf/10.1080/00029890.1979.11994922\",\n      \"title\": \"The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem\\nby D Gale · 1979 · Cited by 401 — DAVID GALE 1. Hex, a game which is probably familiar to many mathematicians, cannot end in a draw. The general Hex Theorem and fixed-point algorithm are\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hex_(board_game)\",\n      \"title\": \"Hex (board game) - Wikipedia\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2001-08-23\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Each player's goal is to form a connected path of their own stones linking their two board edges. The player who completes such a connection wins the game.\\nTo compensate for the first player's advantage, the swap rule (also called the pie rule) is normally used.\\nThis rule allows the second player to choose whether to switch positions with the first player after the first player makes the first move.\\nWhen it is clear to both players who will win the game, it is customary, but not required, for the losing player to resign. In practice, most games of Hex end with one of the players resigning.\\nIn 1979, David Gale published a proof that the determinacy of Hex is equivalent to the two-dimensional Brouwer fixed-point theorem , and that the determinacy of higher-dimensional n -player variants proves the fixed-point theorem in general. [ 26 ]\\n[ edit ]\\nIn Hex without the swap rule on any board of size n x n , the first player has a theoretical winning strategy.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pie_rule\",\n      \"title\": \"Pie rule\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Pie rule\\nThe pie rule, sometimes referred to as the swap rule, is a rule used to balance abstract strategy games where a first-move advantage has been demonstrated.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://sites.google.com/site/boardandpieces/terminology/pie-rule\",\n      \"title\": \"Board and Pieces - Pie Rule\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Board and Pieces - Pie Rule\\nAlso known as the swap rule, the pie rule works to take the first player advantage away and equalize fairness for both players. Using the pie rule,\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://boardgamegeek.com/thread/513596/the-pie-rule-great-or-sucky\",\n      \"title\": \"The pie rule: Great, or sucky?\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2010-04-07\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The pie rule: Great, or sucky?\\nApr 7, 2010 — I first pie rule means that one player makes their first move ; the second player then can either move as normal, or can switch places with the\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.scilit.com/publications/ba28152ee3886c7940f72517dfb80bf4\",\n      \"title\": \"The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem\\nAbstract. (1979). The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem. The American Mathematical Monthly: Vol. 86, No. 10, pp. 818-827.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://cdn.aaai.org/AAAI/2000/AAAI00-029.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"The Game of Hex: An Automatic Theorem Proving ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"While this two-dimensional topological fact may seem\\nobvious, it is not at all trivial. In fact, David Gale\\ndemonstrated that this result is equivalent to the Brouwer\\nfixed-point theorem for 2-dimensional squares (Gale\\n1979). It follows that there exists a winning strategy either\\nfor the first or second player. Using a \\\"strategy stealing\\\"\\nSome Methods of Controlling the Tree Search in\\nChess Programs. Artificial Intelligence 6(4):361-371.\\nBerlekamp, E. R.; Conway, J. H.; and R. K. Guy, R. K.\\n15. Winning Ways for your Mathematical Plays . New\\nYork: Academic press.\\nGale, D. 1979. The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-\\nPoint Theorem. American Mathematical Monthly 86: 818-\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://vigoroushandwaving.wordpress.com/2013/09/30/the-joy-of-hex-and-brouwers-fixed-point-theorem\",\n      \"title\": \"The Joy of Hex and Brouwer's Fixed Point Theorem\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2013-09-30\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The Joy of Hex and Brouwer's Fixed Point Theorem\\nSep 30, 2013 — Gale, David. “The game of Hex and the Brouwer fixed-point theorem.” The American Mathematical Monthly 86, no. 10 (1979): 818-827.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_f3d77f36dffecafp5INUqno9Jy\"\n}",
