          "text": "Excellent! The Notakto paper resolves it:\n\n- \"In the 3x3 misere game, the first player wins by playing in the center, and then wherever the second player plays, the first player plays a knight's move away from that.\" (quoted from Chow's mathoverflow post)\n- \"Kevin Buzzard pointed out that any other first-player move loses: The reason any move other than the centre loses for [the first player to move] in the 3x3 game is that [the second player] can respond with a move diametrically opposite [the first player's] initial move. This makes the centre square unplayable, and then player two just plays the '180 degree rotation' strategy which clearly wins.\"\n\nBut my solver says first player loses on a single board (perfect value O, all first moves O). This contradicts the paper! So there must be a rule discrepancy. Let me think again.\n\nPaper's rules: \"both players make X's on the board. The first player to complete a line of three-in-a-row loses the game.\" Section 2: \"Play ends when every board has a three-in-a-row configuration. The player who completes the last three-in-a-row on the last available board is the loser.\" Hmm wait — \"Play ends when every board has a three-in-a-row configuration.\" On a single board: play ends when the board has a three-in-a-row. The player who completes it loses. So single-board Notakto: first player wins by centre + knight's move.\n\nBut my solver says first player loses. Let me re-examine my solver — maybe the bug is in the terminal rule: `mono(b, X)` returns True if any line of X exists. Winner = other(last). So the completer loses. That matches. Hmm, so why does my solver say all first moves lose?\n\nLet me test manually with the claimed winning strategy: P1 centre (4). P2 plays anywhere, say 0. P1 plays knight's move from 0: 5 or 7. Let's say 5. Board: X at 4,5,0. Lines: 3-4-5 (4,5 but not 3); 2-5-8 (5,8? no 8); 0-4-8 (0,4,8? 8 missing); 0-1-2 (0,1? no); 0-3-6 (0,3? no). No line. P2's turn on {4,5,0}. P2