kto both players must place **X** stones, but my `rec` places `turn` (X or O). So `mono(b, X)` only catches lines formed by P1's stones, and P2's stones are O's, which are inert. The game I solved was actually \"X wins by X line, but completing is loss... X's line loses; O's stones can't form lines\" — a totally wrong rule. That's why H is garbage. I need a rule with an explicit stone symbol.\n\nFix: generalize `solve`/`rec`/`greedy` to use `piece(turn)` mapping; for Notakto, piece = X for both. Simplest: add a `pieces` dict {X: X, O: X} passed to `solve`, default identity; in `rec`, place `pieces[turn]`. Rule functions remain board-based; terminal detection is symbol-agnostic via `mono(b,X)` since all stones are X. Also `rec`'s turn alternation unaffected. Greedy uses `turn` for move application too — must use pieces[turn]. And minimax: value(b, turn) with turn preference for `turn` — fine.\n\nAlso swap_check and mirror_check use pieces implicitly; mirror_check is only for misère rules with distinct symbols, so fine. value_function has no pieces param; for Notakto I need value with pieces. Let me restructure minimally:\n\n- `solve(rule, start, start_turn, pieces=None)` where pieces defaults {X:X, O:O}.\n- `rec`: `p = pieces[turn]`, place p.\n- `greedy`: same.\n- `value_function(rule, pieces)`: place pieces[turn].\n- `swap_check` and `mirror_check` call solve/value_function with default pieces; keep their signatures with an optional pieces but not needed.\n\nThen rule H result should flip to first-player win, matching the paper. Also greedy for H will change.\n\nWait, also check: with pieces both X, the \"mono(b,last)\" defensive branch in misère isn't relevant. R_notakto: terminal if mono(b,X); winner other(last). Good.\n\nAlso note: the paper says \"the first player wins by playing in the center, and then wherever the second player plays, the first player plays a knight's move away from that.\" A knight's move from P2's move might sometimes be occupied or complete