          "text": "The misère baseline: perfect play draw; only first move 4 doesn't lose (0–3,5–8 → O wins; 4 → draw). This matches the arXiv paper: \"the only unbeatable way for the first player is ... centre ... If he starts with edge or corner, he always loses\" (note: their claim covers edge/corner; my solver also shows any non-centre including edges loses).\n\nNow let me collect the primary-source citations. Remaining fetches:\n\n1. arXiv 2208.06795 misère quotes. Fetch ar5iv.\n2. Gale 1979 PDF: fetch the CMC PDF to find whether it mentions the swap rule (probably not; it's about no-draw and Brouwer). Let me fetch and search for \"swap\"/\"pie\"/\"strategy stealing\". If Gale doesn't mention it, the owning source for swap rule is elsewhere: the swap rule in Hex is attributed to Nash? The Wikipedia \"Pie rule\" article's reference [1] says \"first reported in 1909 for a game in the Mancala family\" — I can chase that. But the task says \"(Gale 1979, Hex; also used in Havannah etc.)\". I'll check Gale 1979; if it doesn't own the swap rule, I'll say so (mark as unverified/trace next) and cite what does: the AMS Notices review of \"Hex: The Full Story\" (Hayward & Toft 2020) says the swap rule compensates first-player advantage; the Wiki Pie-rule article says \"In games that cannot end in a draw ... the pie rule theoretically gives the second player a win\". But those aren't primary. Maybe find the actual owner: I believe the swap rule was independently proposed by Nash? Let me search \"swap rule Hex Nash\" or check \"Hex: The Full Story\" book (AMS Notices review by ... it's by the book authors? The review is in AMS Notices 2021, \"Hex: The Full Story\" review by ... maybe by Ryan Hayward? Let me fetch the AMS Notices PDF and quote; it's a book review, i.e., secondary. Hmm.\n\nPrimary source candidates for swap rule:\n- \"Gale, D. (1979)\" might mention \"the swap rule\" in Hex as a way to neutralize the first move advantage. Let me fetch the PDF and grep.\n- For Havannah: official rules (Christian Freeling / mindgames.com?) — \"Havannah rules: the first player places one stone, the second may swap\". There's a page by the inventor; could cite \"Havannah\" official rulebook page. Let me search.\n\n3. Zermelo primary citation: done (Zermelo 1913; Schwalbe & Walker 2001 translation). For the no-draw consequence, I can cite the AIMS \"On Zermelo's theorem\" paper — fetch author/exact statement. Let me webfetch the AIMS page.\n\n4. UCI dataset: fetch page (already have search snippet: \"encodes the complete set of possible board configurations at the end of tic-tac-toe games... Instances 958\"). The exact 626/316/16 breakdown: UCI tic-tac-toe.names says \"positive: x wins; negative: o wins or draw\". The Kaggle-derived 626 positive / 332 negative. The 16 draws specifically: my own enumeration. I can cite the dataset for 958 total and my enumeration for the breakdown. The UCI .names file: let me fetch https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/machine-learning-databases/tic-tac-toe/tic-tac-toe.names to quote the class distribution line. Let me try.\n\n5. OEIS for 255,168: search returned no combined sequence; maybe individual sequences exist: A061528 (O wins 77904). Search OEIS for 131184 and 46080 and 255168 individually. Let me fetch https://oeis.org/search?q=131184&fmt=text, ...=255168..., etc. Perhaps A061527 etc. Let me fetch A061526, A061527, A061528, A061529 blindly? Better to use OEIS search for each number.\n\n6. For the 255,168 owning source: The number first appears... I recall \"The number of possible games of tic-tac-toe is 255,168\" often attributed to a 1970s \"Scientific American\" or to \"D. Michie\"? Let me search \"first computed 255,168\" or \"255,168 Martin Gardner\". Alternatively the enumeration may be owned by \"J. Ferry, n X n tic-tac-toe\" (OEIS link). Let me fetch the Ferry page? It's likely http://www.pisquaredoversix.force9.co.uk/TicTacToe.htm or similar. Let me search \"J. Ferry n X n tic-tac-toe\".\n\n7. Full-board no-line positions \"16\": UCI 958 and my enumeration; also the Kaggle dataset lists 16 negatives that are draws — the dataset itself. Cite UCI + own enumeration.\n\n8. Maker-Breaker: arXiv 2209.12819 quote — have it. Maybe also fetch the arXiv abstract page to confirm title/authors. The search gave title \"Maker-Breaker is solved in polynomial time on hypergraphs of rank 3\". Let me fetch its abstract page for authors. Actually the PDF snippet is enough if I attribute properly; better fetch the abs page.\n\n9. Zermelo consequence for design: \"perfect-play 50/50 balance impossible\" — this is my derivation from determinacy; state as corollary, cite Zermelo/Schwalbe-Walker. Also the pie-rule second-player-win argument: verified computationally for A and B; cite Zermelo + the pie rule sources (official Hex rules?). Let me try to find a primary statement of \"the pie rule theoretically gives the second player a win\". The Wikipedia pie rule article says it; the Hex webapp says it; but the task says primary only. Perhaps \"Hex: The Full Story\" book (Hayward & Toft, CRC Press 2019) states it. I can quote the AMS Notices review or find the book's text. Hmm. Let me check Gale 1979 first.\n\nLet me batch: fetch ar5iv 2208.06795, fetch Gale PDF, fetch AIMS Zermelo page, fetch UCI names file.",
