\"2001-08-23\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Each player's goal is to form a connected path of their own stones linking their two board edges. The player who completes such a connection wins the game.\\nTo compensate for the first player's advantage, the swap rule (also called the pie rule) is normally used.\\nIn 1979, David Gale published a proof that the determinacy of Hex is equivalent to the two-dimensional Brouwer fixed-point theorem , and that the determinacy of higher-dimensional n -player variants proves the fixed-point theorem in general. [ 26 ]\\n[ edit ]\\nIn Hex without the swap rule on any board of size n x n , the first player has a theoretical winning strategy.\\nAll known proofs of this fact are non-constructive, i.e., the proof gives no indication of what the actual winning strategy is. Here is a condensed version of a proof that is attributed to John Nash c. 1949. [ 2 ] The proof works for a number of games including Hex, and has come to be called the strategy-stealing argument .\\nIf in playing this strategy, they are required to play on the cell where an arbitrary move was made, they make another arbitrary move. [ note 1 ] In this way they play the winning strategy with one extra piece always on the board.\\n5.\\nVariants\\n[ edit ]\\nOther connection games with similar objectives but different structures include Shannon switching game (also known as Gale and Bridg-It) and TwixT . Both of these bear some degree of similarity to the ancient Chinese game of Go .\\nRectangular grids and paper and pencil\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cut-the-knot.org/Curriculum/Games/Hex7.shtml\",\n      \"title\": \"The Game of Hex\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"This follows by the strategy stealing argument invented by John Nash. Hence the first player has an advantage in the game. To compensate for this advantage in the applet below, the central cell is blocked for the very first move.\\nUnlike chess or checkers, Hex can't end in a draw . You'll have to do your best to win against the computer.\\nAdvanced level text yourMove - string - Player Move status text thinking - string - Computer Thinking status text whiteWins - string - White Wins status text blackWins - string - Black Wins status text ---------------------------------------------------------->\\nThe applet is courtesy of www.mazeworks.com , Copyright © 2002 Robert Kirkland.\\n1. B. Averbach and O. Chein, Problem Solving Through Recreational Mathematics , Dover, 2000 from 1980 original\\n2. A. Beck, M.N. Bleicher, D. W. Crowe, Excursions into Mathematics , A K Peters, 2000\\n3. C. Browne, HEX Strategy , A K Peters, 2000\\n4. D. Gale, The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem , Am Math Monthly , vol.\\n86, no, 10 (Dec., 1979), 818-827\\n5. M. Gardner, Hexaflexagons and Other Mathematical Diversions , The University of Chicago Press, 1988\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cut-the-knot.org/Curriculum/Games/HexTheory.shtml\",\n      \"title\": \"Hex Can't End in a Draw\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"This was shown by John Nash in 1949 by what since became known as the strategy stealing argument.\\nThe argument goes as follows. Assume there is a winning strategy for the second player. We are then going to show that the first player can utilize this same strategy to win the game.\\nTo make this work, the first player makes a very first move and, in a sense, forgets about it. He imagines now being a second player and, as such, playing the winning strategy on the board with an extra chip of his color. Note that the extra chip can do no harm to this player.\\nThus in the game of Hex, the first player has an advantage. Different stratagems have been devised to minimize this advantage [ Gardner , Browne ]. However, it must be said, that the game is sufficiently complicated for a novice to make use of this advantage. A good second player will have no difficulty to consistently thrush a game opening novice.\\n* What if applet does not run?\\nDiscussion\\n|Contact| |Front page| |Contents| |Interactive activities|\\nCopyright © 1996-2018 Alexander Bogomolny\\nLet's then prove that Hex can't end in a draw. The result is not trivial. David Gale has even shown that the fact that Hex can't end in a draw is equivalent to Brouwer's Fixed Point theorem.\\nI was appalled by the complexity of the 'simple' proof that Hex must have a winner. Here is a much simpler proof that I was told many years ago.\\nImagine the playing board for the game of Hex to be made out of paper. Whenever red moves, he colors the hexagon of his choice red. Whenever blue moves, he cuts out the hexagon of his choice.\\nRepeat this until no one can move any more.\\nPick up the playing board in your hands, holding the two 'red' edges. Pull your hands apart.\\nThe two proofs below appear to elaborate on the above reasoning. One by Anatole Beck [ Beck , pp. 330-338], the other by David Gale [ Gale , pp. 820-822]. Beck makes a reference to the profound Jordan Curve Theorem , which Gale shows to be unnecessary. I'll present a combination of the two.\\n1. B. Averbach and O. Chein, Problem Solving Through Recreational Mathematics , Dover, 2000 from 1980 original\\n2. A. Beck, M.N. Bleicher, D. W. Crowe, Excursions into Mathematics , A K Peters, 2000\\n3. C. Browne, HEX Strategy , A K Peters, 2000\\n4. D. Gale, The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem , Am Math Monthly , vol.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www1.cmc.edu/pages/faculty/MONeill/math188/papers/Gale2.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"A Curious Nim-Type Game - Claremont McKenna College\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"A Curious Nim-Type Game - Claremont McKenna College\\nAlthough it establishes the existence of a winning strategy for PI it is of absolutely no use in finding such a strategy. Here is the argument. There are two possibilities. CASE 1: PI has a winning strategy in which his first move is to select (m, n). CASE 2: If PI selects (m, n) he loses.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www1.cmc.edu/pages/faculty/MONeill/math188/papers/Gale1.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem\\nby D GALE · 1979 · Cited by 392 — The general Hex Theorem and fixed-point algorithm are presented in the final section. We can now state for the third time HEX THEOREM. variants\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/00029890.1979.11994922\",\n      \"title\": \"The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem\\nby D Gale · 1979 · Cited by 399 — (1979). The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem. The American Mathematical Monthly: Vol. 86, No. 10, pp. 818-827.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~hde/hex/hexfaq\",\n      \"title\": \"Hex IAQ\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The first player could \\\"steal\\\" this strategy by making an arbitrary first move and then pretending to be the second player from then on. Whenever the strategy would call for playing on hex he already occupied (e.g. because that was his arbitrary first play), the first player would just make another arbitrary move.\\nstrategy is known?\\n7x7. Jing Yang has written down such a strategy for 7x7 Hex; it is non-trivial (many pages). My program \\\"knows\\\" three winning strategies for 7x7 Hex (each starting with a different first move).\\nWait, isn't 7x7 easy, because you can just play in the middle and\\nwe make it more fair?\\nHave one player set up a position (preferably with not very many stones), specifying which color moves next, and let the other player decide which color to play. Sort of like the \\\"I cut you choose\\\" method for dividing pie.\\n* Martin Gardner. The Scientific American Book of Mathematical Puzzles and Diversions . Simon and Schuster 1959.\\n* S. Even and R. E. Tarjan. A Combinatorial Problem Which Is Complete in Polynomial Space. Journal of the Association for Computing Machinery , volume 23 number 4, October 1976.\\n* David Gale.\\nThe Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-point Theorem. American Mathematical Monthly , number 86, 1979.\\n* Stefan Reisch. Hex ist PSPACE-vollstandig. Acta Informatica , volume 15, pp. 167-191, 1981.\\n* Vadim Anshelevich. The Game of Hex: An Automatic Theorem Proving Approach to Game Programming. Will appear in AAAI-2000 proceedings.\\nWhat is the computational complexity of Hex?\\nHex, or more precisely the decision problem of whether one has a winning move in a given Hex position, is PSPACE-complete. (See the Reisch paper. I haven't checked his proof.)\\nGot any neat Hex problems?\\nA few. Here are some:\\n- - - - - - -\\n- - V - - - -\\n- - - - H - -\\n    - V - - -\\n  - V H V - -\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pie_rule\",\n      \"title\": \"Pie rule\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Pie rule\\nThe pie rule, sometimes referred to as the swap rule, is a rule used to balance abstract strategy games where a first-move advantage has been demonstrated.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_f3d77f36dffecafp5INUqno9Jy\"\n}",
