splits are in the detail section. Hmm, but for standard, the classic 255,168 split is leaf shares 51.4/30.5/18.1. To avoid confusion, in the table I'll show both compactly: \"58.5/28.8/12.7 (unif) | 51.4/30.5/18.1 (leaves)\". That's readable enough. Table cells can hold that.\n\nPer-candidate detail: for each, state rule, interpretation, draws, max/mean length, perfect play + winning first moves, first-move values, random split (both), greedy split, states, verdict.\n\nPart 0 baseline: standard numbers + 16 boards + termination proof.\n\nMethod note: exhaustive enumeration = proof; exact leaf counts; uniform-random probabilities computed by exact rational DP (not sampled); greedy policy = win-now/block-else-uniform with exact DP.\n\nTermination: each move fills an empty cell; stone count strictly increases (solver asserts); max depth ≤ 9 for every rule (solver-reported max ≤ 9; E and H max 7; F max 8); hence no infinite play, no reachable cycles. Also no passing option in any rule set.\n\nCycle check: The state graph is graded by stone count → acyclic. Verified by assertion in solver. Also DFS recursion terminates.\n\nInterpretation notes:\n1. Line-first precedence over fill tiebreak (a move that both completes a line and fills the board is resolved as a line result).\n2. E: maker of the first fully occupied line wins, regardless of symbols.\n3. F: length counts moves after the pre-placement; full-board parity.\n4. Uniform random play: probabilities are over move sequences with each legal move equally likely; leaf shares count each terminal sequence once. These differ.\n5. Greedy: \"win now if possible\" = move that terminates with a win for the mover; \"block\" = move after which the opponent has no immediate winning move; otherwise uniform among all legal moves; if no blocking move exists (double threat), uniform among all legal moves. Greedy uses the same win/terminal definitions per rule (so under misère, \"win now\" means fill-the-board wins where applicable).\n\nPart 2 literature:\n- 255,168 & split: OEIS A061526 (total; a(3)=255168), A061527 (X), A061528 (O), A061529 (draw), all authored by Jim Ferry, 4 May 2001, citing his \"n X n tic-tac-toe (Mathematica programs followed by results)\". These are the oldest traceable owners I found; a pre-2001 original owner was not traced (the number is widely repeated, e.g., Jesper Juul 2003 blog, arXiv 1901.11161 which cites se16.info). Own enumeration reproduces: 131184/77904/46080 and 255168. \n- Perfect-play draw: no owning single source traced; the result is folklore/solved-game; own exhaustive minimax proves it (draw; all 9 first moves draw). Cited as stated in arXiv:2208.06795 (\"It always ends up with draw if both players play their best\") but that paper is not the owner; mark as unverified owner.\n- 958 terminal positions: UCI Tic-Tac-Toe Endgame dataset (D. W. Aha, 1991): \"958 (legal tic-tac-toe endgame boards)\", 65.3% positive (X wins). Own enumeration: 958 (626 X-line, 316 O-line, 16 lineless full). The 16 draws: own enumeration; consistent with dataset.\n- 16 lineless full boards, reachable: own exhaustive DFS (all 46,080 fill sequences; 2,880 per board). Consequence: any rule keeping standard win condition and treating a lineless full board as non-terminal/non-win has draws (standard: 46,080 sequences, 18.06% of leaves). So a no-draw variant must either add a tiebreak for those boards or change the win condition (or hand O a definitional win on fills, as Maker-Breaker does).\n- Misère: Junan Pan, \"Move first, and become unbeatable: Strategy study of different Tic-tac-toe\", arXiv:2208.06795 (2022): quotes: \"The first player can always force a draw if he claims the center for the first move and then choose the square opposite of his opponent's choice. The first player always loses if he claims edge or corner first...\"; and \"the only unbeatable way for the first player is described as above.\" Own exhaustive mirror check: with X opening centre and mirroring every O move, over all 360 O reply sequences: untiebroken misère X 168 / O 0 / draw 192 → X never loses; only centre is non-losing (first-move values: 4=draw, others O). Under C: X wins 360/360; under D: X wins 168, O 192.\n- Notakto: Plambeck & Whitehead, \"The Secrets of Notakto: Winning at X-only Tic-Tac-Toe\", arXiv:1301.1672 (2013): quotes: \"In the 3x3 misere game, the first player wins by playing in the center, and then wherever the second player plays, the first player plays a knight's move away from that.\" and Buzzard: \"The reason any move other than the centre loses ... is that [P2] can respond with a move diametrically opposite ... and then player two just plays the '180 degree rotation' strategy which clearly wins.\" Own exhaustive solve of single-board Notakto: first player wins, only winning first move is centre; max length 7; random second player slightly favoured (51.6%); greedy first 62.8%.\n- Maker-Breaker: definition (Maker wins by owning all of a winning set; Breaker wins if the board fills) and no draws by definition: arXiv:2209.12819 (\"Maker-Breaker is solved in polynomial time on hypergraphs of rank 3\"): \"In a Maker–Breaker positional game, the first player, Maker, wins if Maker occupies all vertices of some hyperedge..., otherwise the second player, Breaker, wins. Therefore, by definition there are no draw plays in Maker–Breaker games.\" and \"It can easily be shown that H is a Maker win, and that the center vertex or any corner vertex is a winning first move for Maker.\" (H = tic-tac-toe hypergraph). Own G solve: Maker X wins, winning first moves centre+corners, matching. Also cite Beck 2008 for the field.\n- Zermelo determinacy: Zermelo 1913 + Schwalbe & Walker 2001 translation; Amir & Evstigneev 2017: \"in any such game either the first player has a winning strategy, or the second player has a winning strategy, or both have unbeatable strategies.\" Consequence: in any finite, no-draw, perfect-information game exactly one player has a forced