            "output": "[2209.12819] Maker-Breaker is solved in polynomial time on hypergraphs of rank 3\n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n      \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\n\n\n    Skip to main content\n  \n  \n  \n\n  \n\n  \n    \n  \n\n  \n    \n      \n      \n      \n    \n  \n\n  \n      \n        \n        \n      \n      Search\n    \n    Submit\n    Donate\n    \n      Log in\n  \n\n\n\n  \n    \n      Search arXiv\n      \n      \n      \n    \n    \n      Press Enter to search · Advanced search\n    \n  \n\n    \n      \n\n\n  \n    \n      Computer Science > Discrete Mathematics\n    \n\n    \n      arXiv:2209.12819 (cs)\n    \n\n\n  \n    \n  [Submitted on 26 Sep 2022 (v1), last revised 11 Jul 2026 (this version, v4)]\n    Title:Maker-Breaker is solved in polynomial time on hypergraphs of rank 3\n    Authors:Florian Galliot, Sylvain Gravier, Isabelle Sivignon            View a PDF of the paper titled Maker-Breaker is solved in polynomial time on hypergraphs of rank 3, by Florian Galliot and 2 other authors\n    View PDF\n    HTML (experimental)\n\n\n\n    \n            Abstract:In the Maker-Breaker positional game, Maker and Breaker take turns picking vertices of a hypergraph $H$, and Maker wins if and only if she possesses all the vertices of some edge of $H$. Deciding the outcome (i.e., which player has a winning strategy) is a PSPACE-complete problem even when restricted to 4-uniform hypergraphs [Galliot, 2025]. As for hypergraphs of rank 3, the linear subcase (i.e., any two distinct edges intersect on at most one vertex) has been solved by obtaining a structural characterization of the outcome and a polynomial-time algorithm to decide it [Kutz, 2005]. A conjecture [Rahman and Watson, 2020] implies that the same results can be obtained for general hypergraphs of rank 3, which we confirm in this paper. We provide a structural characterization of the outcome and a description of both players' optimal strategies, all based on intersections of some key subhypergraph collections. From this, we derive a polynomial-time algorithm, thus closing the complexity gap for Maker-Breaker games relative to the size of the edges. Another corollary of our structural result is that, if Maker has a winning strategy on a hypergraph of rank 3, then she can ensure to win the game within a number of rounds that is logarithmic in the number of vertices.\n    \n\n    \n    \n              \n          Comments:\n          This paper provides a counterexample to a similar result which was incorrectly claimed (arXiv:2209.11202, Theorem 22)\n        \n\n          Subjects:\n          \n            Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM); Combinatorics (math.CO)\n        \n          Cite as:\n          arXiv:2209.12819 [cs.DM]\n        \n        \n           \n          (or \n              arXiv:2209.12819v4 [cs.DM] for this version)\n          \n        \n        \n           \n                        https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2209.12819\n              \n                \n                Focus to learn more\n              \n              \n              \n                                  arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n            \n          \n        \n    \n  \n\n    \n      Submission history From: Florian Galliot [view email]                  [v1]\n        Mon, 26 Sep 2022 16:26:40 UTC (951 KB)\n            [v2]\n        Thu, 10 Nov 2022 16:02:32 UTC (956 KB)\n            [v3]\n        Mon, 24 Mar 2025 15:21:40 UTC (968 KB)\n    [v4]\n        Sat, 11 Jul 2026 15:41:08 UTC (984 KB)\n\n  \n  \n    \n      \n      Full-text links:\n      Access Paper:\n      \n  \nView a PDF of the paper titled Maker-Breaker is solved in polynomial time on hypergraphs of rank 3, by Florian Galliot and 2 other authorsView PDFHTML (experimental)TeX Source\n \n      view license\n    \n        \n    Current browse context:\n  cs.DM\n\n  \n\n      < prev\n    \n      |      \n      next >\n    \n  \n    new\n 