edded code+output, the prose should be concise.\n\nNote on citation numbering: I'll use footnote-style bracketed numbers with a References section.\n\nReferences list:\n[1] OEIS A061526 — Number of complete games of n X n tic-tac-toe (Jim Ferry, 4 May 2001). https://oeis.org/A061526 (accessed 2026-09-21).\n[2] OEIS A061527 — won by X. a(3)=131184.\n[3] OEIS A061528 — won by O. a(3)=77904.\n[4] OEIS A061529 — ending in a draw. a(3)=46080.\n[5] D. W. Aha. Tic-Tac-Toe Endgame database. UCI Machine Learning Repository, 19 Aug 1991. tic-tac-toe.names: 958 legal endgame boards; class distribution 65.3% positive.\n[6] T. E. Plambeck, G. Whitehead. \"The Secrets of Notakto: Winning at X-only Tic-Tac-Toe.\" arXiv:1301.1672 (2013). (Quotes Chow/Buzzard for the single-board result.)\n[7] Junan Pan. \"Move first, and become unbeatable: Strategy study of different Tic-tac-toe.\" arXiv:2208.06795 (2022). (Preprint.)\n[8] F. Galliot, S. Gravier, I. Sivignon. \"Maker-Breaker is solved in polynomial time on hypergraphs of rank 3.\" arXiv:2209.12819 (v4, 2026).\n[9] J. Beck. Combinatorial Games: Tic-Tac-Toe Theory. Encyclopedia of Mathematics and its Applications 114, Cambridge University Press, 2008.\n[10] E. Zermelo. \"Über eine Anwendung der Mengenlehre auf die Theorie des Schachspiels.\" Proc. Fifth Int. Congress of Mathematicians (Cambridge 1912), Vol. 2, CUP, 1913, 501–504.\n[11] U. Schwalbe, P. Walker. \"Zermelo and the Early History of Game Theory.\" Games and Economic Behavior 34(1), 2001, 123–137. doi:10.1006/game.2000.0794 (contains English translation).\n[12] R. Amir, I. V. Evstigneev. \"On Zermelo's theorem.\" Journal of Dynamics and Games 4(3), 2017, 191–194. doi:10.3934/jdg.2017011.\n[13] D. Gale. \"The Game of Hex and the Brouwer Fixed-Point Theorem.\" American Mathematical Monthly 86(10), 1979, 818–827.\n[14] V. Anshelevich. \"The Game of Hex: An Automatic Theorem Proving Approach to Game Programming.\" AAAI-2000, 2000.\n[15] Review of \"Hex: The Full Story\" (R. B. Hayward, B. Toft), Notices of the AMS 68(8), 2021, 1345–. URL ams.org/notices/202108/rnoti-p1345.pdf.\n\nStatements taken from [13]: no-draw theorem and Brouwer equivalence (via [14] and the paper's abstract page). Mark the swap-rule attribution to Gale as unverified.\n\nOK writing now. This is the big one.\n\nI'll also add a \"How to reproduce\" line: save code to solver.py; `python3 solver.py`; Python 3.\n\nLet me make sure the embedded code has the fence with ```python and the output with ```text. The output contains backslashes? No. Fine.\n\nLet me write the file.",
